# Force LF on SQL files that tests read as raw strings.
# A test previously broke only on Windows because the working-tree checkout
# had CRLF line endings (see payment-failed-webhook-alert-rule.test.ts). The
# active migrations whose index blobs are already LF get pinned to LF here so
# fresh Windows checkouts stop introducing CRLF — fixing that failure class.
#
# Scoped deliberately narrow to avoid a repo-wide renormalization in this PR.
# The two exclusions below cover the only committed SQL blobs that still carry
# CRLF in the index; pinning them to eol=lf would queue an 800+/49-file
# renormalization, which we do NOT want in a config-only change. They are left
# byte-for-byte untouched.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Generated pg_dump baseline (CRLF in its committed blob). Not a hand-edited
# fixture; no test depends on its line endings. Leave it exactly as committed
# so this PR does not renormalize ~880 lines of it.
supabase/migrations/00000000000000_baseline_from_prod.sql -text

# Archived pre-baseline legacy migrations (49 files carry CRLF blobs). These
# are never applied (Supabase CLI only runs the immediate migrations root) and
# never read by tests. Leave their line endings untouched.
supabase/migrations/_legacy/** -text

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shell scripts: LF is a CORRECTNESS requirement, not a style preference.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the default on Windows installs) was rewriting every
# .sh to CRLF at CHECKOUT time. Their index blobs are all LF already, so this
# was invisible in `git diff` -- `git ls-files --eol` read `i/lf w/crlf`.
#
# For the .claude/hooks/*.sh enforcement hooks that is fatal, not cosmetic:
# Claude Code execs them directly, so `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` makes the kernel
# look for an interpreter literally named `bash\r`, which does not exist. Every
# hook dies before evaluating a single rule and P14 enforcement silently drops
# to zero. This has already happened once in this repo.
#
# There was no *.sh rule here, which is exactly why the CRLF kept coming back
# after each fresh checkout. Pin it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Pre-existing CRLF blob, excluded to keep this a config-only change (same
# precedent as the SQL exclusions above -- pinning it would queue a
# renormalization diff unrelated to the hook fix). This one IS a genuine
# latent bug: a CRLF shell script breaks under a Linux CI runner. Flagged to
# the mobile agent as a follow-up rather than renormalized here.
mobile/build_apk.sh -text

# Python hooks: `verify_before_stop.py` is launched as `python <path>` and
# `verify-bash-guard.py` as `python3 <path>`, so CRLF is NOT fatal for them
# today the way it is for a shebang-execed .sh. But both are mode 100755 and
# both carry `#!/usr/bin/env python3`, so the identical failure is one
# invocation-style change away. The exposure is latent, the fix is free, and
# every .py index blob is already LF except the one excluded below.
*.py text eol=lf

# Pre-existing CRLF blob; excluded for the same config-only-change reason.
python/services/ai/business/quiz_generator/handler.py -text

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CR-detector fixtures -- MUST NOT be normalized.
#
# `crlf-positive.sh` is deliberately CRLF-infected. It is the POSITIVE control
# that proves the CR detector in verify-hook-patterns.sh can actually fire.
# The `*.sh text eol=lf` rule above would strip its CRs on `git add`, silently
# turning the positive fixture into a second negative fixture -- at which point
# the self-test passes vacuously and we are back to a detector nobody has
# verified. `-text` disables all EOL conversion for this path.
#
# This rule MUST stay after `*.sh text eol=lf` (last matching rule wins).
# Verify with: git check-attr text -- .claude/hooks/__fixtures__/crlf-positive.sh
# Expected:    text: unset
.claude/hooks/__fixtures__/** -text

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Node toolchain pins. Both are consumed by tools that parse the file as a
# bare token (nvm/fnm read .nvmrc; npm reads .npmrc as key=value), and both
# are already committed as LF. Windows checkouts with core.autocrlf=true were
# rewriting .nvmrc to "22\r\n" in the working tree; pin them so the working
# copy matches the committed blob and the Node version cannot drift on a
# trailing CR.
.nvmrc text eol=lf
.npmrc text eol=lf
